// THE HOST'S COMPUTE MEMORY IS A POOL, AND A WORK REQUEST TAKES A SEAT IN IT BEFORE IT RUNS.
//
// gunbc.compute.work_provider_local admitted work by COUNTING LEASE FILES against a policy budget
// of two (compute_cold_build_cap): list the lease directory, compare the count, then create a lease
// for THIS identity. Two things were wrong with that and only the second is about arithmetic.
//
// FIRST, IT WAS NOT ATOMIC. The list and the create are two operations on different names, and the
// create excludes only a second producer of the SAME identity. Two requests with DIFFERENT
// identities both read one lease in flight, both compared 1 < 2, and both created their own lease:
// the cap admitted three. O_EXCL cannot close that, because the thing being over-committed is not a
// name -- it is a quantity, and no exclusive create of any path excludes a quantity.
//
// SECOND, A COUNT IS NOT A CAPACITY. Two producers on a 125 GiB host and two on a 502 GiB host are
// the same policy applied to hosts that differ by a factor of four, and a class that needs a
// gibibyte is charged the same as a release build. What the host actually has is memory, so what is
// reserved is memory -- and the ceiling is compared against a READING of the host rather than
// assumed.
//
// WHAT THIS IS NOT A SECOND AUTHORITY FOR. product.capacity.pool owns the appropriation, the
// encumbrance, the term and the release law; product.capacity.pool_events owns the transitions;
// gunbc.fabric.fabric_event_log owns the linearization -- one compare-and-set per append against the
// partition's head, which is what makes the reservation atomic across competing requests rather
// than merely checked. This module declares WHICH pool (the host's compute appropriation), WHERE it
// is linearized (this host, not the fleet), and joins it to the observation. It coins no capacity
// vocabulary of its own.

// ── WHERE THE LEDGER LIVES, AND WHY NOT THE FLEET'S ────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// gunbc.fabric.fabric_storage_placement places the fabric DB on ONE host and every other host reaches it
// over its served endpoint, which is correct for a fact the FLEET contends on -- an upstream's rate
// limit is metered on the credential, so every host must linearize against one partition.
//
// A HOST'S OWN MEMORY IS NOT SUCH A FACT. The contenders for it are all local, and routing their
// reservation through another machine would mean a local build refusing because a remote host was
// unreachable -- a fail-open in the other direction, since the refusal would not be about capacity
// at all. So the store is the SAME realization (gunbc.fabric.fabric_storage_file_store, whose exclusive
// create supplies the linearization) rooted under this host's own compute root. The partition names
// the host so a ledger copied between machines does not silently answer for the wrong one.
// THE SUBJECT OF A RESERVATION: which ledger, which partition, how much the pool appropriates and
// how much this request takes. It is separated from the instance deliberately, and not only to be
// testable: everything below is arithmetic and transaction over these four facts, while reading
// them off a HostDashboardInstance is a JOIN with the deployment's authority. Fusing the two would
// have made every reservation carry a whole deployment record in order to add two numbers, and
// would have left no seam at which the transaction can be exercised on a host other than the one
// the deployment describes.
//
// THE SUBJECT IS A SEALED VALUE (sole_constructor), AS gunbc.compute.compute_attempt_slots_root ComputeSeatStore IS:
// it holds the two facts only the host's own declaration derives -- WHICH ledger (root) and WHICH partition -- and
// is built in exactly two places, host_capacity_subject (the host resolved by the deployment's authority) and
// host_capacity_subject_in (an instance already in hand, which is how a witness roots the ledger in its own
// directory, as isolated_dashboard_instance does for every instance store). Neither takes a path. What the pool
// appropriates and what a request takes are NOT part of the sealed value: they are arithmetic inputs, supplied to
// the functions that do the arithmetic. The root is a field and field reads are not module-private, so a holder CAN
// read it (declared residual: gunbc.recurring_failure_mode a_slot_store_is_built_over_a_literal_path_by_any_module);
// what is sealed is that a consumer cannot be handed a subject over a root the declaration did not derive.
// THE SUBJECT CARRIES THE BUSINESS KEY, NOT A ROOT. It holds the host's dashboard instance (the one the
// host-to-instance authority resolved, or the isolated one a witness is working in) and which of the
// host's pools it draws from. WHERE the ledger lives is no longer a field: it is derived by the ONE
// generic store mint (gunbc.fabric.fabric_storage_file_store fabric_store_open) from this instance and
// this module's declared chain, so the subject and the store cannot disagree about the root and a holder
// cannot read a root out of it to run the file store by hand. Field reads are still not module-private
// (declared residual: gunbc.recurring_failure_mode a_slot_store_is_built_over_a_literal_path_by_any_module);
// what is sealed is that a consumer cannot be handed a subject over an instance the declaration did not
// resolve, nor a ledger store around a directory its holder chose.
type ComputeCapacitySubject sole_constructor {
  instance: HostDashboardInstance
  partition: PartitionId
}

// THE POOL IS A HOST'S, AND KEYING IT BY THE INSTANCE WAS A MIS-KEYING (side-chat review of
// gunbc#11962 at dd240f0d, 2026-09-21). srv1 declares TWO dashboard instances -- srv1-live and
// srv1-lab -- with different ids and different instance roots, and the memory they would spend is
// ONE machine's. An instance-keyed partition gives each of them its own appropriation and its own
// ledger, so two pools each promise the same bytes and neither can see the other: the exclusion the
// compare-and-set provides is perfect and is about the wrong subject.
//
// So both halves are keyed by the HOST. The partition names the host identity, and the ledger is
// rooted under the instance THAT HOST resolves to -- gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_dashboard_instance
// dashboard_instance_for_host, the same host-to-instance authority the fabric event log already
// uses to decide where a host's log lives. Two instances on one host therefore resolve to one
// partition at one root, which is the single host-scoped authority this fact needs; inventing a new
// host-scoped path would have been a second one.
//
// A HOST THAT AUTHORITY CANNOT RESOLVE RESERVES NOTHING. It is not defaulted to the calling
// instance: that is exactly the fork this fix removes, re-entering through a fallback.
type ComputeSubjectResolution
  = ComputeSubjectResolved { subject: ComputeCapacitySubject, appropriation: Kibibyte }
  | ComputeSubjectHostUnresolved { host: NonEmptyStr }

// THE COMPUTE ROOT THE WORK PROVIDER AND THIS POOL DEMAND: leases, checkouts, per-identity target
// directories (each under that identity's work_dir), the content store and the capacity partition
// all live under it
// (gunbc.compute.work_provider_local reads it from here). Declared at this, the lower of the two
// modules, as an owned-directory target both deployment routes ensure
// (gunbc.roadmap.dashboard_instance_directories); under a root-owned /opt/gunbc a worker's first
// request would otherwise refuse StoreUnavailable at `install -d`. The subdirectories are the
// provider's own and it creates them under this root.
// THE ONE ROW THAT SPELLS COMPUTE'S OWN MEMBER: the provisioned directory (compute_root_directory) and the
// ledger's store chain (compute_capacity_slots) both read it, so renaming it moves the provisioning row and
// the mint together and they cannot drift.
data compute_root_member: NonEmptyStr = "compute"

fn compute_root_directory(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> OwnedDirectoryTarget {
  dashboard_instance_shared_directory(instance: instance, member: "compute-root" as NonEmptyStr, child: compute_root_member as String)
}

// THE LEDGER'S PROVISIONING ROWS, DERIVED FROM THE SAME CHAIN THE MINT READS: the declared root and the
// objects and heads areas fabric_store_open stands the store on (fabric_store_area_paths, applied to
// compute_capacity_slots) are demanded as owned directories, so the substrate creates them before any
// reservation opens the store. Renaming a member of the chain moves the mint and these rows together;
// nothing else spells the path.
fn compute_capacity_directories(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> List<OwnedDirectoryTarget> {
  let areas = fabric_store_area_paths(members: compute_capacity_slots, instance: instance)
  [
    dashboard_instance_shared_directory(instance: instance, member: "compute-capacity-root" as NonEmptyStr, child: join(map(compute_capacity_slots, s => s as String), "/")),
    compute_capacity_area(instance: instance, member: "compute-capacity-objects" as NonEmptyStr, path: areas.objects),
    compute_capacity_area(instance: instance, member: "compute-capacity-heads" as NonEmptyStr, path: areas.heads),
  ]
}

fn compute_capacity_area(instance: HostDashboardInstance, member: NonEmptyStr, path: FilePath) -> OwnedDirectoryTarget {
  OwnedDirectoryDeclared {
    member: member,
    path: path,
    owner: instance.directory_owner,
    mode: DirectoryOwnerAllEveryoneTraverse,
    group_adoption: ForeignOwnershipRefuses,
  }
}

fn compute_root(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> NonEmptyStr {
  owned_directory_path(t: compute_root_directory(instance: instance)) as String as NonEmptyStr
}

// THE ONE DECLARATION OF THE CAPACITY LEDGER'S LOCATION: the member chain under the host instance's root
// that the ledger sits on -- compute's own declared member, then this ledger's. It is DATA in the shape
// the ONE generic store mint reads (List<NonEmptyStr>), so the capacity ledger is not a special case of
// the fabric family: it is an instance of the same rule, obtained from the same mint, with no mint copy
// and no second record. The provisioning row for the compute directory (compute_root_directory, and the
// census rows that ask for it) reads the same compute_root_member row the chain's head is; the
// ledger's chain adds this module's member and nothing else.
data compute_capacity_slots: List<NonEmptyStr> = [compute_root_member, "capacity" as NonEmptyStr]

fn compute_capacity_root(host_instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> NonEmptyStr {
  fabric_store_declared_root(members: compute_capacity_slots, root: host_instance.instance_root) as String as NonEmptyStr
}

fn compute_memory_partition(host: NonEmptyStr) -> PartitionId {
  join(["compute-memory-", host as String], "") as PartitionId
}

// WHICH OF THE HOST'S POOLS A RESERVATION DRAWS FROM. A host carries TWO memory pools and they are
// separate on purpose (bold-bee-114 ruling 2026-10-03, on a finding from the G5 dogfood lane): an
// agent session that held a seat in the BUILD pool while the builds it requests need that same pool
// is a self-starvation shape -- two live sessions take both build slots and every build they cause
// refuses as a full pool. A typed refusal there only turns a deadlock into a refusal storm. So the
// session lease and the builds it causes draw from different partitions, each with its own
// appropriation, under the SAME ledger realization, linearization and release law -- one capacity
// authority, two subjects, not a second authority.
//
// THE SESSION POOL'S SLOT COUNT IS SUPPLIED, NOT DECLARED HERE, because its authority is the
// belt's admission count (gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_session_placement passes it) and this module may not import
// the belt. A count supplied by the caller is a fact of the subject, exactly like the request.
//
// DECLARED FRONTIER (DESIGN 3c): the two pools are adjudicated separately against the machine's
// live availability, so neither sees the other's granted-but-untouched memory -- the double-promise
// compute_backing_ceiling closes WITHIN a pool is open BETWEEN pools. Trigger: the per-host read of
// outstanding grants' resident share named at compute_backing_ceiling, whose second consumer is the
// cross-pool sum.
type HostCapacityPool
  = ComputeBuildPool
  | AgentSessionPool { slots: Nat }

fn host_capacity_pool_partition(pool: HostCapacityPool, host: NonEmptyStr) -> PartitionId {
  match pool {
    ComputeBuildPool => compute_memory_partition(host: host)
    AgentSessionPool { slots: _ } => join(["session-memory-", host as String], "") as PartitionId
  }
}

fn host_capacity_pool_slots(pool: HostCapacityPool) -> Nat {
  match pool {
    ComputeBuildPool => compute_pool_slots
    AgentSessionPool { slots } => slots
  }
}

// THE JOIN WITH THE DEPLOYMENT, and the only function in this module that knows what an instance is.
fn compute_capacity_subject(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> ComputeSubjectResolution {
  host_capacity_subject(instance: instance, pool: ComputeBuildPool)
}

fn host_capacity_subject(instance: HostDashboardInstance, pool: HostCapacityPool) -> ComputeSubjectResolution {
  match dashboard_instance_for_host(host: instance.host_identity as String) {
    DashboardInstanceHostUnknown { host: h } => ComputeSubjectHostUnresolved { host: h as NonEmptyStr }
    DashboardInstanceForHost { instance: host_instance } => {
      let ledger = host_capacity_subject_in(requesting: instance, host_instance: host_instance, pool: pool)
      ComputeSubjectResolved { subject: ledger.subject, appropriation: ledger.appropriation }
    }
  }
}

// THE SECOND MINT: the subject for a host instance already in hand. host_capacity_subject reaches it through the
// deployment's host-to-instance authority; a witness reaches it with an isolated instance, which is the only way a
// test roots the ledger in its own directory without a path-taking constructor.
fn host_capacity_subject_in(requesting: HostDashboardInstance, host_instance: HostDashboardInstance, pool: HostCapacityPool) -> ComputeCapacityLedger {
  ComputeCapacityLedger {
    subject: ComputeCapacitySubject {
      instance: host_instance,
      partition: host_capacity_pool_partition(pool: pool, host: requesting.host_identity),
    },
    appropriation: host_capacity_pool_appropriation(instance: host_instance, pool: pool),
  }
}

// WHAT A MINT RETURNS ONCE THE HOST IS KNOWN: the sealed subject and the appropriation the declaration derives for it.
// The mint is total, so it has no refusal arm; the unresolved-host refusal belongs to the resolution above.
type ComputeCapacityLedger { subject: ComputeCapacitySubject, appropriation: Kibibyte }

// THE LEDGER'S STORE, FROM THE ONE MINT: this module's chain row and the instance the subject was minted
// for -- never a root read off the subject and never a directory the caller picked. The mint lists the
// declared root and stands up the two areas (the prepare this module used to do by hand through
// fabric_storage_file_root_ensure, which is deleted), so the store is either standing with its cause or
// refused with its cause: no caller projects the refusal away.
type ComputeCapacityStoreStanding
  = ComputeCapacityStoreReady { store: FabricStorageBinding }
  | ComputeCapacityStoreUnready { area: String, detail: String }

fn compute_capacity_store(subject: ComputeCapacitySubject) -> ComputeCapacityStoreStanding {
  match fabric_store_open(members: compute_capacity_slots, instance: subject.instance, entry_mode: none) {
    FabricStoreUnready { area: a, detail: d } => ComputeCapacityStoreUnready { area: a, detail: d }
    FabricStoreReady { store: ops } => ComputeCapacityStoreReady { store: FabricStorageLocalFiles { operations: ops } }
  }
}

// ── WHAT THE HOST HAS, READ RATHER THAN SPELLED ────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// TWO FIGURES AND THEY ANSWER DIFFERENT QUESTIONS. MemTotal is the machine's installed memory and
// does not move, which is what a ledger needs: an appropriation whose ceiling changed between two
// folds would make the same chain of events add up differently each time it was read. MemAvailable
// is what is free RIGHT NOW including everything outside this pool -- the runner slices, the
// sessions, the page cache the kernel can hand back -- and it is a LIVE FLOOR, not a ceiling.
//
// Both are needed and neither substitutes for the other. The pool alone would admit work while
// another tenant had eaten the machine; MemAvailable alone would admit work whose own ledger says
// the memory is already promised, because memory a reservation holds but has not yet touched is
// still available to read.
type HostMemoryObservation
  = HostMemoryObserved { total: Kibibyte, available: Kibibyte }
  | HostMemoryUnobserved { detail: String }

fn observe_host_memory() -> HostMemoryObservation {
  let read = linux.Procfs.ReadMeminfo()
  if !read.success {
    HostMemoryUnobserved { detail: "/proc/meminfo could not be read on this host" }
  } else {
    match parse_proc_meminfo(text: read.value) {
      MeminfoFieldAbsent { field: f } => HostMemoryUnobserved { detail: join(["/proc/meminfo carries no readable ", f as String, " line"], "") }
      MeminfoParsed { meminfo: m } => HostMemoryObserved { total: m.mem_total, available: m.mem_available }
    }
  }
}

// ── THE APPROPRIATION, AND WHAT IT REPLACES ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE SHARE OF THE HOST THIS PROVIDER MAY COMMIT, denominated in the bytes the old count was
// implicitly worth: compute_cold_build_cap was two producers, and each producer runs under the
// instance's worker memory ceiling, so two ceilings is EXACTLY the memory the deleted row admitted.
// Nothing is widened by this change and a class that needs less than a ceiling now admits more
// requests than a count ever could.
//
// DECLARED FRONTIER (DESIGN 3c), STATED BECAUSE IT IS THE HONEST LIMIT OF THIS ROW: charging this
// appropriation against the host's OTHER commitments -- the runner slice, the sessions slice, the
// fixed overhead -- is gunbc.ci.ci_runner_placement's conservation wall, and this appropriation is
// DECLARED rather than derived from it. What keeps that from being fail-open is the pair of
// observations below: an appropriation larger than the machine refuses outright, and a request is
// refused when the machine's live availability cannot back it whatever the ledger says.
data compute_pool_slots: Nat = 2

fn compute_memory_appropriation(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> Kibibyte {
  host_capacity_pool_appropriation(instance: instance, pool: ComputeBuildPool)
}

fn host_capacity_pool_appropriation(instance: HostDashboardInstance, pool: HostCapacityPool) -> Kibibyte {
  kibibyte(count: kibibyte_count(k: kibibyte_from_byte_size_floor(b: instance.dispatch_worker_memory_max)) * host_capacity_pool_slots(pool: pool))
}

// THE UNIT'S CEILING IS THE RESERVED AMOUNT, and that identity is the whole point rather than a
// convenience. A reservation of X that caps the unit at Y is arithmetic about a number nothing
// enforces: if Y > X the pool's total is a fiction, and if Y < X the pool refuses work the host
// could have run. So one figure serves both, and it is the instance's worker memory ceiling --
// the existing safety limit, unchanged and now also the thing being counted.
//
// THE CEILING IS WHAT A CLASS RESERVES WHEN IT HAS NO GRANT, and it is no longer what every request
// reserves. The per-work-class amount (a claims run does not need a release build's ceiling) is
// gunbc.compute.work_class_grant work_class_grant, which resolves to THIS figure for any class
// without enough samples on the provider's own route -- the fail-closed arm, which over-reserves
// and never under-reserves. A request's amount is therefore supplied to compute_reserve rather than
// read here; this function only names the ceiling the grant falls back to and never exceeds.
fn compute_memory_ceiling(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> Kibibyte {
  kibibyte_from_byte_size_floor(b: instance.dispatch_worker_memory_max)
}

// A COMPUTE SEAT IS QUIESCENCE-REQUIRED AND THAT IS THE SAFE ARM, NOT A LIMITATION. A running cargo
// cannot be told its lease lapsed -- there is no fence a compute unit honours -- so a term that runs
// out frees nothing, and product.capacity.pool holds the encumbrance until the seat is RELEASED.
// A worker that dies wedges its own share rather than silently handing it to the next arrival,
// which is the only direction that cannot oversubscribe a machine. Recovering a wedged seat is an
// explicit release naming why, which is the same entry completion and cancellation take.
fn compute_lease_policy(term_seconds: Nat) -> LeasePolicy {
  LeasePolicy { maximum_duration_seconds: term_seconds, release_law: QuiescenceRequired }
}

// THE BACKING RELATION, AND IT IS THE WHOLE REASON THE TWO OBSERVATIONS ARE NOT INDEPENDENT CHECKS.
//
// The first revision compared the pool's committed total against the appropriation in one place and
// the machine's live availability against ONE request in another. Both checks passed and the pair
// still double-promised (side-chat review of gunbc#11962 at dd240f0d, 2026-09-21): with a 52 GiB
// appropriation, 30 GiB available and two 26 GiB requests, each request sees 26 <= 30 and each fits
// the appropriation, so BOTH admit -- because neither sees the other's granted-but-not-yet-consumed
// memory. The compare-and-set was doing its job perfectly on a question that was missing a term.
//
// The fix is not a third check. The pool already computes the committed total INSIDE the linearized
// fold, so the machine's reading belongs in the SAME comparison: the ceiling this attempt is
// adjudicated against is the smaller of the declared appropriation and what the host says is free.
// Then "everything this pool has promised, plus what I am asking for, must fit in what the machine
// actually has" is one predicate evaluated in one CAS'd step, and the second 26 GiB request is
// refused as a full pool because 26 + 26 > 30.
//
// WHAT THIS ADMITS AGAINST IS AN ESTIMATE, AND IT IS NOT A NO-OOM GUARANTEE. MemAvailable is the
// kernel's own ESTIMATE of what a new workload could obtain without swapping, it moves between the
// reading and the grant, and nothing here constrains the processes outside this pool. So the claim
// this relation supports is "this pool does not knowingly promise more than the machine reported",
// which is a real property and is strictly weaker than "no admitted workload will be OOM-killed".
// The unit's own MemoryMax is what bounds a single workload; this is what stops the POOL from
// over-promising. Conflating the two would be the rung inflation DESIGN 4b forbids.
//
// WORST-CASE-SOUND, AND CONSERVATIVE, AND SAYING SO. MemAvailable already excludes whatever the
// pool's outstanding grants have actually touched, so a grant that is fully resident is counted
// twice -- once in the machine's reading and once in the committed total. That errs toward refusing
// work the host could have run, which is the direction a safety admission must err in when the term
// it needs is unobserved. The exact relation needs the RESIDENT share of outstanding grants, which
// is observable -- each grant runs in a named transient unit with its own cgroup -- and is not
// observed here. DECLARED FRONTIER (DESIGN 3c), trigger: a per-host read of the compute units'
// current usage, whose first consumer is this fold; until it lands the conservative bound stands
// and the cost is stated rather than hidden.
//
// A CEILING THAT MOVES BETWEEN ATTEMPTS IS SAFE BY CONSTRUCTION HERE, which is why this is a ceiling
// and not a pre-check. product.capacity.pool ruled that replay does not adjudicate the ceiling --
// an event's admission happened when it was written -- so a dip in availability cannot make a
// historical acquire refuse the fold and poison the partition. It makes the pool over-committed,
// which refuses new acquires until it drains. That is the correct consequence of a reduction.
fn compute_backing_ceiling(appropriation: Kibibyte, available: Kibibyte) -> Kibibyte {
  if kibibyte_count(k: available) < kibibyte_count(k: appropriation) { available } else { appropriation }
}

fn compute_memory_root_pool(subject: ComputeCapacitySubject, ceiling: Kibibyte) -> Pool<Memory, Kibi> {
  pool_of(
    identity: subject.partition as String as NonEmptyStr,
    ceiling: ceiling,
    replenishment: NoReplenishment,
    release_law: QuiescenceRequired,
  )
}

// ── THE RESERVATION ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// EVERY ARM IS TYPED AND NONE OF THEM WIDENS. Insufficient capacity is ComputeCapacityFull and says
// so; a machine whose live availability cannot back the request is ComputeHostBelowFloor and is a
// DIFFERENT fact with a different remedy (wait, or find out who else is on the host); an
// unobservable host reserves nothing at all rather than reserving against a guess.
type ComputeReservation
  = ComputeReserved { grant: LeaseGrant, partition: PartitionId, amount: Kibibyte, store: FabricStorageBinding }
  | ComputeCapacityFull { wire: String }
  | ComputeHostBelowFloor { requested: Kibibyte, available: Kibibyte }
  | ComputeReservationRefused { detail: String }

data compute_seat_attempts: Nat = 3
data compute_partition_read_budget: Nat = 4096

// THE APPROPRIATION IS CHECKED AGAINST THE MACHINE BEFORE ANY SEAT IS TAKEN. A host too small for
// the declared share is a configuration error that would otherwise present as an OOM kill during a
// build, which is the same information arriving hours later and destructively.
fn compute_reserve(instance: HostDashboardInstance, reference: NonEmptyStr, term_seconds: Nat, request: Kibibyte) -> ComputeReservation {
  host_capacity_reserve(instance: instance, pool: ComputeBuildPool, reference: reference, term_seconds: term_seconds, request: request)
}

fn host_capacity_reserve(instance: HostDashboardInstance, pool: HostCapacityPool, reference: NonEmptyStr, term_seconds: Nat, request: Kibibyte) -> ComputeReservation {
  match host_capacity_subject(instance: instance, pool: pool) {
    ComputeSubjectHostUnresolved { host: h } =>
      ComputeReservationRefused { detail: join(["no dashboard instance is declared for host ", h as String, ", so this host has no compute capacity authority to reserve against"], "") }
    ComputeSubjectResolved { subject: subject, appropriation: appropriation } =>
      compute_reserve_on(subject: subject, appropriation: appropriation, request: request, reference: reference, term_seconds: term_seconds)
  }
}

// THE DECISION TAKES THE OBSERVATION AS A VALUE, AND THE DOOR ABOVE TAKES THE READING.
//
// It used to read /proc/meminfo itself, which made the admission arithmetic untestable except
// against whatever the host happened to be doing: the headroom control had to DERIVE its fixture
// from the live reading, and a fixture derived from the thing under test is not a fixture. It was
// wrong in both directions -- on a lightly loaded host the derived appropriation exceeded MemTotal
// and the cell refused before reaching the comparison it existed to make, and on a busy one it
// passed for reasons unrelated to the relation (side-chat review of gunbc#11962 at 725d9c58).
//
// So the boundary is here: this fold decides admission from a supplied observation, and
// compute_reserve supplies the real one. DESIGN 3's pairing obligation is discharged by an
// inhabitance claim that observe_host_memory really produces a well-formed reading of this machine,
// and by the srv1 receipts, where the whole path runs for real.
fn compute_reserve_on(subject: ComputeCapacitySubject, appropriation: Kibibyte, request: Kibibyte, reference: NonEmptyStr, term_seconds: Nat) -> ComputeReservation {
  match observe_host_memory() {
    HostMemoryUnobserved { detail: d } => ComputeReservationRefused { detail: join(["the host's memory could not be observed, so nothing was reserved: ", d], "") }
    HostMemoryObserved { total: total, available: available } =>
      compute_reserve_against(subject: subject, appropriation: appropriation, request: request, reference: reference, term_seconds: term_seconds, total: total, available: available)
  }
}

fn compute_reserve_against(
  subject: ComputeCapacitySubject,
  appropriation: Kibibyte,
  request: Kibibyte,
  reference: NonEmptyStr,
  term_seconds: Nat,
  total: Kibibyte,
  available: Kibibyte,
) -> ComputeReservation {
  if kibibyte_count(k: appropriation) > kibibyte_count(k: total) {
    ComputeReservationRefused {
      detail: join([
        "the compute appropriation is ", to_string(value: kibibyte_count(k: appropriation)),
        " KiB and this host has ", to_string(value: kibibyte_count(k: total)),
        " KiB installed, so the share was never backed by the machine",
      ], ""),
    }
  } else if kibibyte_count(k: available) < kibibyte_count(k: request) {
    ComputeHostBelowFloor { requested: request, available: available }
  } else {
    match compute_capacity_store(subject: subject) {
      ComputeCapacityStoreUnready { area: a, detail: d } => ComputeReservationRefused { detail: join(["the capacity ledger is not standing at ", a, ": ", d], "") }
      ComputeCapacityStoreReady { store: store } =>
        match now_epoch_seconds() {
          Absent => ComputeReservationRefused { detail: "the clock could not be read as epoch seconds" }
          Present { value: now } => compute_seat(subject: subject, store: store, request: request, reference: reference, now: now, term_seconds: term_seconds, ceiling: compute_backing_ceiling(appropriation: appropriation, available: available))
        }
    }
  }
}

// A REFUSED ACQUIRE IS NOT AUTOMATICALLY A FULL POOL. The carrier renders every pool refusal into
// SeatFull, so the reason survives only in the wire; a DuplicateReference reported as capacity sent
// an operator looking for a busy host while srv1 had 400 GiB free (2026-09-21). The marker is read
// from the authority that writes it (product.capacity.pool_events pool_full_wire), never re-spelled
// here, and anything else is a ledger refusal with its text carried through.
fn compute_seat(subject: ComputeCapacitySubject, store: FabricStorageBinding, request: Kibibyte, reference: NonEmptyStr, now: EpochSecs, term_seconds: Nat, ceiling: Kibibyte) -> ComputeReservation {
  match fabric_seat_acquire(
    store: store,
    partition: subject.partition,
    root: compute_memory_root_pool(subject: subject, ceiling: ceiling),
    actor: reference,
    request: SeatRequest { reference: reference, amount: request, at: now, term_seconds: term_seconds },
    policy: compute_lease_policy(term_seconds: term_seconds),
    attempts: compute_seat_attempts,
    budget: compute_partition_read_budget,
  ) {
    SeatGranted { grant: g, generation: _, attempts_used: _ } => ComputeReserved { grant: g, partition: subject.partition, amount: request, store: store }
    SeatFull { wire: w, attempts_used: n } =>
      if starts_with(s: w, prefix: pool_full_wire) {
        ComputeCapacityFull { wire: join([w, " (attempt ", to_string(value: n), ")"], "") }
      } else {
        ComputeReservationRefused { detail: join(["the capacity ledger refused the acquire: ", w, " (attempt ", to_string(value: n), ")"], "") }
      }
    other => ComputeReservationRefused { detail: seat_acquisition_wire(a: other) }
  }
}

// ── THE RELEASE, WHICH IS THE ONLY WAY CAPACITY COMES BACK ─────────────────────────────────────
//
// COMPLETION AND CANCELLATION ARE THE SAME TRANSITION WITH DIFFERENT REASONS, which is why the
// reason is carried rather than encoded in two functions: the pool does not care why a holder
// stopped, and a caller that had to choose between two spellings would eventually pick the wrong
// one on the path it exercises least -- which is always the cancellation path.
//
// A RELEASE THAT DID NOT LAND IS NOT A RELEASE. The append is retried against a moving head and
// exhaustion is its own arm; nothing here reports a return of capacity it did not observe.
// NOTHING-TO-RELEASE IS ITS OWN ARM, BECAUSE A SEAT THE POOL DOES NOT HOLD IS NOT A FAILED RELEASE.
// Three states, not two: this caller returned the seat; there was no seat to return; the ledger
// could not return it. Folding the middle into the last manufactures an incident -- outcome.json
// says the seat is STILL CHARGED and carries the stuck-reservation remedy, and an operator chases a
// seat that recovery already returned. It is the same distinction gunbc.compute.work_provider_local
// makes at compute_settle_attempt, where AttemptUnsettleable projects to HostRecordWritten
// deliberately: a loss means something was OWED and not done, and a producer that owed nothing
// cannot have lost anything. Here it was unmade, and by a CATCH-ALL rather than by a decision --
// `other => ComputeReleaseRefused` in the fold below, which reads as exhaustive while being a
// default, so SeatNotHeld arrived at the refusal arm nobody wrote it into.
//
// THE THREE PRE-GRANT ARMS BELOW WERE ALREADY SAYING IT IN PROSE. Each rendered `nothing to
// release: ...` into a REFUSED detail, which is the right sentence in the wrong constructor: a
// reservation that was never granted has nothing to return, and every reader deciding on the arm
// rather than the text saw a failure.
type ComputeRelease
  = ComputeReleased { partition: PartitionId, reference: NonEmptyStr, reason: NonEmptyStr }
  | ComputeNothingToRelease { detail: String }
  | ComputeReleaseRefused { detail: String }

fn compute_release(reservation: ComputeReservation, reason: NonEmptyStr) -> ComputeRelease {
  match reservation {
    ComputeCapacityFull { wire: w } => ComputeNothingToRelease { detail: join(["nothing to release: ", w], "") }
    ComputeHostBelowFloor { requested: _, available: _ } => ComputeNothingToRelease { detail: "nothing to release: the host was below its live memory floor" }
    ComputeReservationRefused { detail: d } => ComputeNothingToRelease { detail: join(["nothing to release: ", d], "") }
    ComputeReserved { grant: g, partition: p, amount: _, store: store } =>
      compute_release_on(store: store, partition: p, appropriation: kibibyte(count: 0), reference: g.reference, reason: reason)
  }
}

// THE ONE RELEASE PATH, CHECKED. It reads, folds, PROPOSES the release against the folded pool and
// appends only what the pool admits -- the mirror of the acquire. That check is load-bearing rather
// than defensive: a reference the pool is not holding, appended anyway, would not fail at the
// append; it would fail at every later READ, when the fold hits UnknownEncumbrance and refuses the
// WHOLE partition.
//
// THE OPERATOR'S RECOVERY DOOR IS NOT HERE, AND IT IS NO LONGER ABSENT: it is one layer up, at
// gunbc.compute.work_provider_local compute_recover_cli, and it comes back through THIS function
// rather than growing a release path of its own. What had to exist before one could ship was named
// in this annotation and by gunbc.recurring_failure_mode a_held_compute_seat_has_no_in_corpus_discharge
// -- a termination decision bound to the unit, an attempt-to-unit binding that cannot release
// another attempt's live reservation, and a settled state a retry consumes. All three are now
// present, and the reason the door is up there rather than here is the same reason this fold takes
// a store and a partition rather than an instance: the pool knows about encumbrances, and deciding
// that a particular attempt is dead is not a fact about a pool.
//
// WHAT HAS NOT CHANGED is why a recovery may not simply free on doubt. Shipping one with any of
// those three missing would FREE SEATS THAT SHOULD NOT BE FREED, which is worse than having none:
// every failure here keeps the charge and the host merely under-admits.
//
// THE APPROPRIATION PASSED HERE IS NOT LOAD-BEARING AND THE ZERO IS NOT A FABRICATED READING. product.capacity.pool
// ruled that replay never adjudicates the ceiling, so a release is decided by whether the ledger
// HOLDS the reference and by nothing about capacity; a reservation released from its own provider
// carries no subject to read an appropriation from, and inventing one for it would be a number with
// no consequence. Where a subject is at hand it is passed; where none is, zero is passed, and zero
// cannot admit anything -- which is the right direction for a value nothing should be deciding on.
fn compute_release_on(
  store: FabricStorageBinding,
  partition: PartitionId,
  appropriation: Kibibyte,
  reference: NonEmptyStr,
  reason: NonEmptyStr,
) -> ComputeRelease {
  match now_epoch_seconds() {
    Absent => ComputeReleaseRefused { detail: "the clock could not be read as epoch seconds" }
    Present { value: now } =>
      match fabric_seat_release(
        store: store,
        partition: partition,
        root: pool_of(
          identity: partition as String as NonEmptyStr,
          ceiling: appropriation,
          replenishment: NoReplenishment,
          release_law: QuiescenceRequired,
        ),
        reference: reference,
        reason: reason,
        at: now,
        attempts: compute_seat_attempts,
        budget: compute_partition_read_budget,
      ) {
        SeatReleased { reference: r, id: _ } => ComputeReleased { partition: partition, reference: r, reason: reason }
        SeatNotHeld { reference: r, wire: w } =>
          ComputeNothingToRelease { detail: join(["the pool is not holding ", r as String, ", so there is no seat to return: ", w, ". A double release reaches here -- recovery returning a seat the producer then releases again is the ordinary case -- and it is not a ledger failure"], "") }
        SeatReleaseContended { attempts: a } => ComputeReleaseRefused { detail: seat_release_wire(r: SeatReleaseContended { attempts: a }) }
        SeatReleaseRefused { step: st, reason: rs } => ComputeReleaseRefused { detail: seat_release_wire(r: SeatReleaseRefused { step: st, reason: rs }) }
      }
  }
}

// WHEN THE LEDGER ACQUIRED A GIVEN REFERENCE, read rather than remembered.
//
// A holder-death proof has to be bound to the holder's identity, and one leg of that binding is a TIME:
// the instant the hold was acquired, against the instant the host's current boot began. Until now the
// ledger could answer "is this reference held?" only by trying to release it, and "how much room is
// left?" by folding the partition -- never "when did THIS encumbrance begin?". A consumer that needed
// that instant had its own clock, which is the instant of the reading, and comparing a reading's own
// instant to a boot says nothing about when the hold began. That is the unbound inference gunbc.recurring_failure_mode
// hold_recovered_by_age_not_observed_holder_death exists to stop, and the arm that would stop it
// (gunbc.systemd_unit_invocation_liveness unit_reboot_holder_liveness) cannot be written without this
// question being answerable. So this is the same chain-minus-append walk the standing reading already
// runs, asking a second question of the same fold -- a second question, not a second authority: the
// answer is the folded pool's own LeaseTerm.acquired_at for that reference.
//
// THE CEILING TRAVELS WITH THE POOL ROOT AND CHANGES NOTHING HERE. product.capacity.pool ruled that
// replay never adjudicates the ceiling, so the folded terms do not depend on it; the ceiling is passed
// because the root type carries one, and it is the caller's real appropriation rather than a minted
// number.
//
// NOT HELD IS ITS OWN ANSWER. A reference with no term is not held, and that is reported as such: a zero
// would read as "acquired at the epoch", older than every hold, which would make any boot look like it
// began after the acquisition -- a default that manufactures death evidence. More than one term naming one
// reference is a pool defect and is refused, not resolved by picking one.
type ComputeSeatAcquisition
  = SeatAcquisitionRead { reference: NonEmptyStr, acquired_at: EpochSecs, generation: Nat }
  | SeatAcquisitionNotHeld { reference: NonEmptyStr }
  | SeatAcquisitionRefused { detail: String }

fn compute_seat_acquisition_at(store: FabricStorageBinding, partition: PartitionId, ceiling: Kibibyte, reference: NonEmptyStr) -> ComputeSeatAcquisition {
  match fabric_seat_acquisition_of(
    store: store,
    partition: partition,
    root: pool_of(
      identity: partition as String as NonEmptyStr,
      ceiling: ceiling,
      replenishment: NoReplenishment,
      release_law: QuiescenceRequired,
    ),
    reference: reference,
    budget: compute_partition_read_budget,
  ) {
    SeatAcquisitionObserved { reference: r, acquired_at: at, generation: g } => SeatAcquisitionRead { reference: r, acquired_at: at, generation: g }
    SeatAcquisitionNoTerm { reference: r } => SeatAcquisitionNotHeld { reference: r }
    SeatAcquisitionAmbiguous { reference: r, terms: c } =>
      SeatAcquisitionRefused { detail: join(["the pool carries ", to_string(value: c), " terms for ", r as String, "; which one governs this reference is not decided here"], "") }
    SeatAcquisitionUnobserved { step: st, reason: rs } => SeatAcquisitionRefused { detail: join(["the acquisition instant could not be read (", st, "): ", rs], "") }
    SeatAcquisitionStoreRefused { cause: c } => SeatAcquisitionRefused { detail: event_log_refusal_wire(cause: c) }
  }
}

// ── WAS THIS REFERENCE'S CHARGE RETURNED, AND CAN THE HISTORY SAY SO? ──────────────────────────
//
// WHY THIS READ EXISTS. The settle pass closes a slot only when it can name the holder's death
// (gunbc.compute.attempt_lifecycle.recovery_trigger requires an ended unit for a launching attempt), and the
// holder's death is dated from the acquisition instant, which lives in the ledger's CURRENT terms
// (compute_seat_acquisition_at above). Once a charge has been returned the instant is gone from the folded
// pool, and a slot that becomes reachable afterwards -- carried in from a root a moved instance no longer
// reads, say -- can never be closed: it is neither held nor closeable. This read answers the one question
// that state turns on, POSITIVELY: does this partition's own history show the charge acquired and then
// returned?
//
// THE HISTORY, NOT THE CURRENT POOL. The answer is taken from the chain in append order, so a reference that
// was acquired, released, and acquired again is reported as still held: the release that matters is the one
// that closed the acquisition that is STILL open. Answering from the current pool would answer a different
// question ("is it held now") and would let a stale release in an old generation stand in for the current
// one.
//
// NEVER-ACQUIRED IS ITS OWN ANSWER, and it is not "returned". A slot whose reference has no acquisition
// event anywhere in the partition has had no charge to return; retiring it on the strength of an empty
// history would destroy the only record of an attempt whose acquire had not been written yet. That case is
// reported apart so the caller keeps the slot.
//
// ONE QUESTION OF THE SAME FOLD, NOT A SECOND AUTHORITY. This consumes the partition read and the chain walk
// exactly as compute_seat_acquisition_at does; it adjudicates nothing about the pool, and it never writes.
type ComputeSeatCharge
  = SeatChargeReturned { reference: NonEmptyStr, acquired_at: EpochSecs, returned_at: EpochSecs, by: ChargeReturnCause, generation: Nat }
  | SeatChargeStillHeld { reference: NonEmptyStr, acquired_at: EpochSecs }
  | SeatChargeNeverAcquired { reference: NonEmptyStr }
  | SeatChargeUnproven { detail: String }

// WHAT CLOSED THE CHARGE IS A FACT ABOUT THE LEDGER, NOT A STRING SOMEONE CHOSE.
// gunbc.product.capacity.pool_events has exactly two ways a held charge stops being held: a release, which
// carries its reason, and a settlement, which carries the settled amount instead. Naming those as arms is what
// makes "a return with no reason" UNWRITABLE rather than merely unreachable.
//
// THE SETTLEMENT AMOUNT CARRIES THE LEDGER'S OWN FACT, NOT A WIDER COPY OF IT. The ledger declares
// `PoolSettled { reference: NonEmptyStr, actual: Nat }` (product/capacity/pool_events.dag), so that amount is
// never absent and never negative; an optional here would invent a state the ledger cannot produce, and the
// wire arm for it would be prose about a row that cannot exist - DESIGN 5's fabricated output. Its unit is not
// a guess either: the same ledger hands that count to `pool_settle(... actual: Measure { count: a })`, and the
// partition this reads belongs to `Pool<Memory, Kibi>` (compute_memory_root_pool above), so the count is a
// KiB count and it is carried as this module's own measure, Kibibyte - the same carrier every other amount in
// this module renders through.
type ChargeReturnCause
  = ReturnedByRelease { reason: NonEmptyStr }
  | ReturnedBySettlement { actual: Kibibyte }

// THE SCAN IS A SUM, NOT A RECORD OF THREE OPTIONALS. The record shape - acquired_at?, returned_at?,
// release_reason? - stood in for three states while permitting others the model rules out: a return with no
// cause, a cause with no return. The tell that the shape was wrong was that answering it required inventing a
// string for a case that cannot happen, which DESIGN 5 forbids ("no plausible output is fabricated") and
// answers more strongly by construction: the unmakeable states get no constructor.
//   ScanOpen                     the acquisition is in the window and nothing has closed it
//   ScanReturned                 it closed, with the ledger row that closed it
//   ScanReturnedUnacquired       the ledger shows the close and does not show the open (a settlement naming a
//                                reference this history never shows acquired) - kept distinct, because the
//                                reading must not claim an acquisition it did not see
type SeatChargeScan
  = ScanOpen { acquired_at: EpochSecs }
  | ScanReturned { acquired_at: EpochSecs, returned_at: EpochSecs, by: ChargeReturnCause }
  | ScanReturnedUnacquired { returned_at: EpochSecs, by: ChargeReturnCause }

// ONE STEP FOR EVERY REFERENCE AT ONCE. DESIGN 2: when several demands share a least common ancestor in
// state, carry, rewire or SHARE the first value. The settle pass needs this answer for every listed slot, and
// asking it once per slot reads and walks one partition K times over - a slots-by-events fold, and DESIGN 6b
// fixes a proven cost-shape defect regardless of how small the realized numbers are today. So the fold carries
// a map from reference to scan, keyed by the reference each event itself names.
//
// A lapse clears the whole map, which is what a lapse does to a pool: it clears every hold, so from that event
// forward the partition holds no acquisition for any reference (SeatChargeNeverAcquired says "the partition
// holds no acquisition for it", which is the true statement about the history after a lapse and not a claim
// that none was ever written; the settle decision treats that answer exactly as it treats a still-held or
// unproven one - the ordinary trigger, never a retirement - so nothing is retired on the strength of a lapse).
fn seat_charge_scan_cleared() -> Map<String, SeatChargeScan> { empty_map() }

fn seat_charge_scan_all_step(state: Map<String, SeatChargeScan>, wrapped: ChainEnvelope<PoolEvent>) -> Map<String, SeatChargeScan> {
  let event = wrapped.event
  match event.payload {
    PoolAcquired { reference: r, amount: _, term_seconds: _ } =>
      map_insert(m: state, key: r as String, value: ScanOpen { acquired_at: event.recorded_at })
    PoolReleased { reference: r, reason: why } =>
      match map_lookup(m: state, key: r as String) {
        Absent => state
        Present { value: s } =>
          match s {
            ScanOpen { acquired_at: at } =>
              map_insert(m: state, key: r as String, value: ScanReturned { acquired_at: at, returned_at: event.recorded_at, by: ReturnedByRelease { reason: why } })
            // A release naming a reference this scan has already closed is the ledger repeating a fact, not a
            // second closing of the charge: the first close keeps its own cause.
            _ => state
          }
      }
    PoolSettled { reference: r, actual: actual } =>
      match map_lookup(m: state, key: r as String) {
        Absent =>
          // The settlement row is in the ledger and the acquisition is not, so the scan records the close
          // without inventing the open. seat_charge_of_scan answers that state as not-acquired, which is the
          // answer the pass can act on - no release can be fenced on an instant the history does not contain.
          map_insert(m: state, key: r as String, value: ScanReturnedUnacquired { returned_at: event.recorded_at, by: ReturnedBySettlement { actual: kibibyte(count: actual) } })
        Present { value: s } =>
          match s {
            ScanOpen { acquired_at: at } =>
              map_insert(m: state, key: r as String, value: ScanReturned { acquired_at: at, returned_at: event.recorded_at, by: ReturnedBySettlement { actual: kibibyte(count: actual) } })
            _ => state
          }
      }
    PoolLapsed => seat_charge_scan_cleared()
    PoolUpstreamObserved { remaining: _, term_seconds: _ } => state
    PoolResetObserved { reset_at: _ } => state
  }
}

// THE PARTITION READ AND WALKED ONCE FOR THE WHOLE PASS. An unread history is one refusal for the whole
// reading - it does not degrade into "never acquired" for some references and "returned" for others, which is
// what a per-reference read on a shared partition risks when one read succeeds and another does not.
type SeatChargeReading
  = ChargeScansRead { scans: Map<String, SeatChargeScan>, generation: Nat }
  | ChargeScansUnreadable { detail: String }

// WHY THERE IS NO CEILING PARAMETER. The bound is a property of the read, not of the caller:
// compute_partition_read_budget is the same budget the acquisition read uses, and a caller-chosen
// ceiling over one partition's history would be a second, unenforced number sitting beside it. A chain
// that cannot be read to its end is a refusal, never "not returned".
fn compute_seat_charge_scans(store: FabricStorageBinding, partition: PartitionId) -> SeatChargeReading {
  match event_log_read_partition(store: store, partition: partition, budget: compute_partition_read_budget) {
    PartitionReadRefused { cause: c } => ChargeScansUnreadable { detail: event_log_refusal_wire(cause: c) }
    PartitionReadOk { head: _, walk: walk } =>
      match walk {
        ChainIncomplete { missing: m, newest_first_so_far: _ } =>
          ChargeScansUnreadable { detail: join(["the partition chain is missing event ", m as String, "; an incomplete history cannot prove a charge was returned"], "") }
        ChainBudgetExhausted { at: a } =>
          ChargeScansUnreadable { detail: join(["the partition chain exceeded the read budget at ", a as String, "; the history was not read to its end"], "") }
        // THE GENERATION THE READING SITS AT IS PART OF THE PROOF, not decoration: it is the number a
        // settlement quotes when it says a charge was already returned. A walked chain that cannot name it
        // refuses rather than handing back a number that looks like one (DESIGN 5: a path that cannot answer
        // gives a typed, located diagnostic). chain_generation reports Present for every ChainWalked, so this
        // refusal arm is a door that does not open today; it is written because a fabricated default is the
        // kind of thing that quietly becomes true the day someone widens the walk.
        ChainWalked { oldest_first: xs } =>
          match chain_generation(walk: walk) {
            Absent => ChargeScansUnreadable { detail: "the walked chain reported no generation; a charge whose ledger generation cannot be read is not something this reading will call returned" }
            Present { value: generation } =>
              ChargeScansRead { scans: fold(xs, init: seat_charge_scan_cleared(), f: fn(acc, wrapped) { seat_charge_scan_all_step(state: acc, wrapped: wrapped) }), generation: generation }
          }
      }
  }
}

// ONE REFERENCE ANSWERED OUT OF THAT FOLD, with the same coproduct the pass already decides on. The
// generation named on a returned charge is the generation this reading was taken at - one value for one
// reading of one partition, which is what a settlement record should quote.
fn seat_charge_of_scan(reading: SeatChargeReading, reference: NonEmptyStr) -> ComputeSeatCharge {
  match reading {
    ChargeScansUnreadable { detail: d } => SeatChargeUnproven { detail: d }
    ChargeScansRead { scans: scans, generation: generation } =>
      match map_lookup(m: scans, key: reference as String) {
        Absent => SeatChargeNeverAcquired { reference: reference }
        Present { value: scan } =>
          match scan {
            ScanOpen { acquired_at: at } => SeatChargeStillHeld { reference: reference, acquired_at: at }
            ScanReturned { acquired_at: at, returned_at: back, by: cause } =>
              SeatChargeReturned { reference: reference, acquired_at: at, returned_at: back, by: cause, generation: generation }
            ScanReturnedUnacquired { returned_at: _, by: _ } =>
              // The ledger shows this reference closed and never shows it opened. Nothing can be released
              // against it (a release is fenced on an acquisition instant the history does not contain), so the
              // answer the pass can act on is the one it gives when nothing was acquired - while the scan still
              // holds the two situations apart, so no reader is told an acquisition was seen when it was not.
              SeatChargeNeverAcquired { reference: reference }
          }
      }
  }
}

// THE SINGLE-REFERENCE QUESTION IS THE SAME FOLD, ANSWERED ONCE. It stays because a caller that genuinely has
// one reference (a witness, an operator report) should not have to build the pass's fold, and because two scan
// semantics sitting side by side is exactly the second-authority mistake this reading exists to avoid.
fn compute_seat_charge_returned(store: FabricStorageBinding, partition: PartitionId, reference: NonEmptyStr) -> ComputeSeatCharge {
  seat_charge_of_scan(reading: compute_seat_charge_scans(store: store, partition: partition), reference: reference)
}

// HOW A CLOSED CHARGE'S CAUSE IS WRITTEN DOWN. A display function, not a decision: the cause reached here is
// the ledger's own row, and the only choice made is how to say it.
fn charge_return_cause_wire(c: ChargeReturnCause) -> String {
  match c {
    ReturnedByRelease { reason: why } => join(["released by: ", why as String], "")
    ReturnedBySettlement { actual: a } => join(["closed by a settlement row of ", to_string(value: kibibyte_count(k: a)), " KiB"], "")
  }
}

fn compute_seat_charge_wire(c: ComputeSeatCharge) -> String {
  match c {
    SeatChargeReturned { reference: r, returned_at: back, generation: g, acquired_at: _, by: cause } => join([r as String, ": charge returned at ", to_string(value: back), " (ledger generation ", to_string(value: g), ", ", charge_return_cause_wire(c: cause), ")"], "")
    SeatChargeStillHeld { reference: r, acquired_at: _ } => join([r as String, ": charge still held"], "")
    SeatChargeNeverAcquired { reference: r } => join([r as String, ": the partition holds no acquisition for it"], "")
    SeatChargeUnproven { detail: d } => join(["the charge's fate could not be read: ", d], "")
  }
}

// ── WHAT THE HOST IS CARRYING, WITHOUT TAKING FROM IT ──────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE AGGREGATE READING A RECEIPT CARRIES: what the ledger says is committed on this host, beside
// what the machine says is free. The two are independent measurements of one quantity and a
// receipt that carried only the first would be a ledger agreeing with itself (DESIGN 5: a
// measurement copied from the same tree is not an oracle). Their DISAGREEMENT is the signal --
// committed far above the machine's own shortfall means the pool is over-reserving each class, and
// the reverse means work is running outside the pool.
type ComputeCapacityStanding
  = ComputeCapacityStandingRead { committed: Kibibyte, ceiling: Kibibyte, headroom: Kibibyte, observed_available: Kibibyte, observed_total: Kibibyte }
  | ComputeCapacityStandingUnread { detail: String }

// THE STANDING IS A FACT ABOUT THE POOL, NOT ABOUT ANY REQUEST, so it reads the subject at the
// ceiling: no field of the standing fold consults the subject's request.
fn compute_capacity_standing(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> ComputeCapacityStanding {
  match compute_capacity_subject(instance: instance) {
    ComputeSubjectHostUnresolved { host: h } => ComputeCapacityStandingUnread { detail: join(["no dashboard instance is declared for host ", h as String], "") }
    ComputeSubjectResolved { subject: subject, appropriation: appropriation } => compute_capacity_standing_on(subject: subject, appropriation: appropriation)
  }
}

fn compute_capacity_standing_on(subject: ComputeCapacitySubject, appropriation: Kibibyte) -> ComputeCapacityStanding {
  match observe_host_memory() {
    HostMemoryUnobserved { detail: d } => ComputeCapacityStandingUnread { detail: d }
    HostMemoryObserved { total: total, available: available } =>
      match now_epoch_seconds() {
        Absent => ComputeCapacityStandingUnread { detail: "the clock could not be read as epoch seconds" }
        Present { value: now } =>
          match compute_capacity_store(subject: subject) {
            ComputeCapacityStoreUnready { area: a, detail: d } =>
              ComputeCapacityStandingUnread { detail: join(["the capacity ledger is not standing at ", a, ": ", d], "") }
            ComputeCapacityStoreReady { store: ledger } =>
              match fabric_seat_observe(
                store: ledger,
            partition: subject.partition,
            root: compute_memory_root_pool(subject: subject, ceiling: appropriation),
            at: now,
            budget: compute_partition_read_budget,
          ) {
            SeatRoomObserved { headroom: h, generation: _ } =>
              ComputeCapacityStandingRead {
                committed: kibibyte(count: kibibyte_count(k: appropriation) - kibibyte_count(k: h)),
                ceiling: appropriation,
                headroom: h,
                observed_available: available,
                observed_total: total,
              }
            SeatFullObserved { generation: _ } =>
              ComputeCapacityStandingRead {
                committed: appropriation,
                ceiling: appropriation,
                headroom: kibibyte(count: 0),
                observed_available: available,
                observed_total: total,
              }
            SeatStandingUnobserved { step: s, reason: why } => ComputeCapacityStandingUnread { detail: join(["the capacity ledger could not be read at ", s, ": ", why], "") }
              SeatStandingStoreRefused { cause: _ } => ComputeCapacityStandingUnread { detail: "the capacity ledger refused the read" }
            }
          }
      }
  }
}

fn compute_reservation_wire(r: ComputeReservation) -> String {
  match r {
    ComputeReserved { grant: g, partition: p, amount: a, store: _ } =>
      join(["reserved ", to_string(value: kibibyte_count(k: a)), " KiB on ", p as String, " as ", g.reference as String, " fence=", g.fence.grant as String, " expires_at=", to_string(value: g.expires_at)], "")
    ComputeCapacityFull { wire: w } => join(["capacity full: ", w], "")
    ComputeHostBelowFloor { requested: rq, available: av } =>
      join(["host below floor: ", to_string(value: kibibyte_count(k: rq)), " KiB requested, ", to_string(value: kibibyte_count(k: av)), " KiB available"], "")
    ComputeReservationRefused { detail: d } => join(["refused: ", d], "")
  }
}

fn compute_capacity_standing_wire(s: ComputeCapacityStanding) -> String {
  match s {
    ComputeCapacityStandingRead { committed: c, ceiling: cl, headroom: h, observed_available: av, observed_total: tt } =>
      join([
        "committed ", to_string(value: kibibyte_count(k: c)), " KiB of ", to_string(value: kibibyte_count(k: cl)),
        " KiB (headroom ", to_string(value: kibibyte_count(k: h)), " KiB); host reports ", to_string(value: kibibyte_count(k: av)),
        " KiB available of ", to_string(value: kibibyte_count(k: tt)), " KiB installed",
      ], "")
    ComputeCapacityStandingUnread { detail: d } => join(["unread: ", d], "")
  }
}
